Endpoint Verification is the native helper that lets Google's Endpoint
Verification Chrome extension report device attributes (serial number,
disk encryption, OS version, hostname, ...) to the Google Workspace
admin console.

This slackbuild repackages Google's Debian binary (it is proprietary and
is not redistributed here) and installs it, like upstream, under
/opt/google/endpoint-verification. The init script, systemd unit and
maintainer scripts of the .deb are not used. device_state.sh is patched
to recognise Slackware and to read /run/host/os-release inside a Flatpak.

1. Download the .deb into this directory; the helper checks its sha256
   and discards the file if it does not match:

     ./download-deb.sh

   By hand it is endpoint-verification_1765828494702-842239260_amd64.deb,
   from the DOWNLOAD_x86_64 url of the .info file.

2. Build and install as root:

     ./google-endpoint-verification.SlackBuild
     installpkg /tmp/google-endpoint-verification-*_SBo.tgz

3. Startup (nothing is done automatically). Add this to /etc/rc.d/rc.local,
   which must be executable, and run the same lines once as root now:

     if [ -x /opt/google/endpoint-verification/bin/device_state.sh ]; then
       /opt/google/endpoint-verification/bin/device_state.sh init > /opt/google/endpoint-verification/var/lib/device_attrs
     fi

   It stores the serial number and disk encryption state, which need root.

4. Flatpak Google Chrome, as your normal user (not root):

     /opt/google/endpoint-verification/bin/ev-flatpak-setup
     flatpak override --user --filesystem=/opt/google/endpoint-verification:ro com.google.Chrome

   Restart Chrome, install the Endpoint Verification extension from the
   Chrome Web Store and click "Sync Now".

   If Chrome cannot start the helper, retry with the fallback, which runs
   it on the host through flatpak-spawn. It needs the broad permission
   org.freedesktop.Flatpak, which lets Chrome run commands on the host:

     /opt/google/endpoint-verification/bin/ev-flatpak-setup --spawn
     flatpak override --user --talk-name=org.freedesktop.Flatpak com.google.Chrome

   To undo: ev-flatpak-setup --remove, then flatpak override --user --reset
   com.google.Chrome.

Notes:
- Google only supports Debian and Ubuntu. On Slackware os_firewall (ufw only)
  is empty and screen_lock_secured (GNOME/Cinnamon only) is UNKNOWN. os_version
  is the real VERSION_ID of /etc/os-release. Upstream aborts when ufw.conf
  is missing; the patch makes those values empty instead.
- device_state.sh uses absolute tool paths; the build warns about any missing.
- removepkg does not delete /opt/google/endpoint-verification/var/lib/device_attrs
  nor the per-user files; remove them by hand.
